\u0644 \u0627\u0644\u0627\u062a\u0635\u0627\u0644", "cs": "Kontaktn\u00ed osoba", "de": "Kontaktperson", "en": "Contact person", "es": "Persona de contacto", "sv": "Kontaktperson"}}, "affiliations": [{"id": "04d836q62", "name": "TU Wien", "identifiers": [{"identifier": "04d836q62", "scheme": "ror"}, {"identifier": "grid.5329.d", "scheme": "grid"}, {"identifier": "0000 0004 1937 0669", "scheme": "isni"}]}]}, {"person_or_org": {"type": "personal", "name": "Matteo, Maffei", "given_name": "Maffei", "family_name": "Matteo", "identifiers": [{"identifier": "0000-0001-8061-1685", "scheme": "orcid"}]}, "affiliations": [{"id": "04d836q62", "name": "TU Wien", "identifiers": [{"identifier": "04d836q62", "scheme": "ror"}, {"identifier": "grid.5329.d", "scheme": "grid"}, {"identifier": "0000 0004 1937 0669", "scheme": "isni"}]}]}], "title": "eThor: Practical and Provably Sound Static Analysis of Ethereum Smart Contracts (artifacts)", "publisher": "TU Wien", "publication_date": "2020-05-08", "subjects": [{"id": "http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf?1.2", "subject": "Computer and information sciences", "scheme": "FOS"}], "languages": [{"id": "eng", "title": {"en": "English"}}], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.1145/3372297.3417250", "scheme": "doi", "relation_type": {"id": "ispartof", "title": {"ar": "\u062c\u0632\u0621 \u0645\u0646", "cs": "Je sou\u010d\u00e1st\u00ed (\u010deho)", "de": "Ist Teil von", "en": "Is part of", "es": "Es parte de", "sv": "\u00c4r del av"}}, "resource_type": {"id": "publication-conferencepaper", "title": {"de": "Konferenzbeitrag", "en": "Conference Paper"}}}, {"identifier": "10.14722/ndss.2018.23082", "scheme": "doi", "relation_type": {"id": "cites", "title": {"ar": "\u064a\u0633\u062a\u0634\u0647\u062f \u0628\u0640", "cs": "Cituje (co)", "de": "Zitiert", "en": "Cites", "es": "Citas", "sv": "Citerar"}}, "resource_type": {"id": "publication-conferencepaper", "title": {"de": "Konferenzbeitrag", "en": "Conference Paper"}}}], "rights": [{"id": "gpl-3.0-or-later", "title": {"en": "GNU General Public License v3.0 or later"}, "description": {"en": "Permissions of this strong copyleft license are conditioned on making available complete source code of licensed works and modifications, which include larger works using a licensed work, under the same license. Copyright and license notices must be preserved. Contributors provide an express grant of patent rights."}, "props": {"url": "https://spdx.org/licenses/GPL-3.0-or-later", "scheme": "spdx"}}, {"id": "cc0-1.0", "title": {"en": "Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal"}, "description": {"en": "CC0 waives copyright interest in a work you've created and dedicates it to the world-wide public domain. Use CC0 to opt out of copyright entirely and ensure your work has the widest reach."}, "icon": "cc-cc0-icon", "props": {"url": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/", "scheme": "spdx"}}, {"id": "mit", "title": {"en": "MIT License"}, "description": {"en": "A short and simple permissive license with conditions only requiring preservation of copyright and license notices. Licensed works, modifications, and larger works may be distributed under different terms and without source code."}, "props": {"url": "https://opensource.org/license/mit", "scheme": "spdx"}}], "description": "<p>This dataset contains the software and benchmarks accompanying the paper \"eThor: Practical and Provably Sound Static Analysis of Ethereum Smart Contracts\" (published at the <a href=\"https://www.sigsac.org/ccs/CCS2020/\" rel=\"noopener\">ACM Conference on Computer and Communications Security (CCS) 2020</a>).</p>\n<p><code>ethor-sources.zip</code> and <code>horst-sources.zip</code> contain the sources for our smart contract analyzer eThor and the HoRSt framework on which it is based.</p>\n<p><code>ethor.jar</code> and <code>horst.jar</code> are the compiled versions of eThor and HoRSt.</p>\n<p><code>control-flow-reconstruction-tool.zip</code> contains the sources for the tool used to reconstruct the control flows.</p>\n<p><code>contracts.zip</code> contains the contracts in the reentancy benchmark with redacted bytecodes (720 contracts). See below for unredaction.<br><code>contracts-with-reconstructed-control-flow.zip</code> contains the non-trivial contracts in the benchmark contracts with reconstructed control flow and redacted bytecodes (605 contracts). See below for unredaction.<br><code>contracts-with-sources.txt</code> contains the addresses of contracts in the reentrancy benchmark, to whose Solidity source code we had access to during assessment of the ground truth (601 contracts).<br><code>results.csv</code> contains the classification of contracts and ground truth.</p>\n<p><code>case-study-data.zip</code> contains the data set for the functional correctness case study.</p>\n<h3>Provenance, Licenses, Recovery of Original Files</h3>\n<p>The software developed developed by the authors of this resource (<span class=\"codespan__pre-wrap\"><code>ethor-sources.zip</code></span>, <span class=\"codespan__pre-wrap\"><code>horst-sources.zip</code></span> and <span class=\"codespan__pre-wrap\"><code>control-flow-reconstruction-tool.zip</code>, <code>ethor.jar</code>, <code>horst.jar</code></span>) are licensed under GPL 3 or later.</p>\n<p>All contract bytecodes in our benchmark were obtained from Ethereum Mainnet.<br><br>We redacted the bytecodes of the the smart contracts in our benchmarks due to licensing concerns. To obtain the file that we used for our experiements, first obtain the hex-encoded bytecode of any contract <code>XYZ</code> and place it next to <code>XYZ.json</code> in a file <code>XYZ.hex</code>.&nbsp; Then executed<br><br><code>sed -i \"s/REMOVED-DUE-TO-LICENSING-CONCERNS/$(cat XYZ.hex)/\" XYZ.json</code><br><br>The bytecode for a contract <code>XYZ</code> can be, for example, obtained via etherscan, <a class=\"theme markdown__link\" href=\"https://etherscan.io/address/&lt;address&gt;#code\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://etherscan.io/address/XYZ#code</a> (where XYZ has to be replaced with the actual address). Please note that all our tools ingest deployed/runtime bytecodes, not contract creation codes. Etherscan was also where we obtained the sources mentioned in <code>contracts-with-sources.txt</code>.<br><br><span class=\"codespan__pre-wrap\"><code>contracts.zip</code></span>, <span class=\"codespan__pre-wrap\"><code>contracts-with-reconstructed-control-flow.zip</code>, <code>contracts-with-sources.txt</code>, and <code>results.csv</code> are distributed under CC0.<br></span><br>The addresses of&nbsp;the contracts in our benchmark were gathered from <a class=\"theme markdown__link\" href=\"https://goo.gl/kFNHy3\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://goo.gl/kFNHy3</a> (taken from \"ZEUS: Analyzing Safety of Smart Contracts\", NDSS '2018).</p>\n<p>The solidity files in <span class=\"codespan__pre-wrap\"><code>case-study-data.zip</code></span> were derived from <span class=\"codespan__pre-wrap\"><code>https://github.com/OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts/blob/9fdc91758dbd958a28a238a87c71b21e501a47c7/contracts/math/SafeMath.sol</code></span>&nbsp;and are distributed under MIT.</p>\n<h3>Limitations on Reproducibility</h3>\n<p>The SMT-LIB files produced by eThor are not 100% deterministic, as the order of some statements is&nbsp;not fixed. This should not change the semantics of the respective file but may impact solver performance.</p>"}, "custom_fields": {}, "access": {"record": "public", "files": "public", "embargo": {"active": false, "reason": null}, "status": "open"}, "files": {"enabled": true, "default_preview": "README.md", "order": [], "count": 11, "total_bytes": 51439321, "entries": {"control-flow-reconstruction-tool.zip": {"id": "83003443-b0f5-4999-a4e8-10a1b07c9dfe", "checksum": "md5:78edb13894b5314f1ca1b4137f7471e1", "ext": "zip", "size": 1279928, "mimetype": "application/zip", "storage_class": "L", "key": "control-flow-reconstruction-tool.zip", "metadata": {}, "access"